# Build context is the repository root for both images (`context: ..`), so everything excluded
# here is everything Docker does not have to send on every build.
#
# `**/` is not decoration. Docker matches these patterns against the path relative to the context
# root, so a bare `node_modules/` excludes only the TOP-LEVEL one -- and this is a pnpm workspace,
# where the node_modules directories that matter are nested (`apps/api/node_modules`). Leaving
# them in the context copies the host's install on top of the image's own: on Windows those are
# pnpm shims and absolute symlinks (`/c/Users/...`) that mean nothing inside Linux. Measured cost
# of getting this wrong (defect D2): the image builds, and then `nest build` dies with
#   Cannot find module '/app/apps/api/node_modules/@nestjs/cli/bin/nest.js'
# Same reasoning for `dist/` and `.venv/`: host artifacts, built for the host.
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
**/build/
**/.next/
**/.turbo/
**/.venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/storage/
**/tmp/
**/*.log

# Host build caches. These are the nastiest kind of context leak: a foreign incremental cache
# makes the image's own compiler skip work and emit a PARTIAL `dist/` that still reports success
# (defect D3 -- the container then died with `Cannot find module './app.module'`).
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.eslintcache

# Credentials never enter an image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Version control and documentation: not runtime inputs, and .git alone is worth excluding.
.git/
.gitignore
openspec/
odd/
.atl/
.pi/
